;; A graduated experiment's GRADUATED[] shim is a MIGRATION WINDOW, not a
;; permanent alias, and this fixture pins what happens when the window closes.
;;
;; The lifecycle has three states for a name:
;;   live      -- an EXPERIMENTS[] row; `--enable=<name>` turns the feature on
;;   graduated -- the row is gone, the name is in GRADUATED[]; `--enable=<name>`
;;                is accepted as a no-op with TUR-W0063, so a downstream
;;                `build.tur` or `~/.config/turmeric/experiments.tur` that opted
;;                in keeps compiling across the graduation boundary
;;   retired   -- the name is out of GRADUATED[] too, one minor line later;
;;                `--enable=<name>` is the hard TUR-E0310 an unknown name gets
;;
;; `sealed-opaque` graduated 2026-08-17 (v0.34.0) and was retired at 0.38.0,
;; with refined, cycle-gc, jit, global-state, write-frames and checked-reads.
;; It stands in for all seven here: what is being pinned is the REGISTRY's
;; third state, not anything about `:sealed` -- so one fixture is the right
;; number, and it would still be if the specific name in `flags` were swapped
;; for another retired one.
;;
;; This replaces sealed-opaque-graduated-enable-noop, which pinned the SECOND
;; state for the same name.  That promise expired on purpose; deleting its
;; fixture without pinning the state that succeeded it would have left the
;; retirement itself untested, which is how a shim quietly comes back.
;;
;; The program is deliberately trivial: `--enable` is rejected during argument
;; handling, before anything here is read.
(defn main [] : int
  (println "unreachable -- the flag is refused first")
  0)
